00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.610
Nuclear energy is one of the cleanest, most
efficient, and most available sources of power

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.809
on earth.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:19.640
To generate one kilowatt hour of energy—the
amount an American household

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:25.890
consumes in 48 minutes—nuclear power plants
only emit 12 grams of carbon dioxide— enough

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.800
to fill about three two-liter soda bottles.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.009
Meanwhile, to produce the same amount of energy,
coal

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:38.109
plants emit 820 grams of CO2—about a full
bathtub’s worth.

00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:42.750
Factoring in the environmental cost
of production, nuclear energy is cleaner than

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.120
hydropower, than geothermal, than solar, than
really

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.479
any energy source except wind.

00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:51.579
But that doesn’t necessarily mean nuclear
is the long-term

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:57.229
solution for the world because nuclear material
is perhaps the most poisonous substance on

00:00:57.229 --> 00:00:58.229
earth.

00:00:58.229 --> 00:01:02.370
Two times in history have nuclear power plants
leaked significant amount of radiation—in

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.410
1986
in Chernobyl, Ukraine and in 2011 in Fukushima,

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.410
Japan.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:12.570
31 people died in Chernobyl with at
least a further 4,000 expected to contract

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.130
early lethal cancer due to the radiation.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.900
Fukushima was
better contained with only two deaths, both

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.370
unrelated to radiation, and only 130 early
cancer

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:27.570
deaths expected, but additionally, each site
still today has massive exclusion zones where

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.490
humans cannot live due to ongoing radiation.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.550
Hundreds of thousands of people were displaced
from their homes and will never be allowed

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.550
to return.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:43.710
The economic damage of Chernobyl is
estimated at nearly $250 billion dollars—significantly

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.050
more than the GDP of Ukraine.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.560
The
Fukushima disaster, meanwhile, having taken

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.390
place is a much more populated and developed
area, is estimated to set Japan back over

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.690
$500 billion dollars—a full 10% of their
GDP.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.510
In
addition, uranium, the element most commonly

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.460
used in nuclear reactors, is not in limitless
supply.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.129
Using present-day extraction methods, there’s
only about a 230 year supply of uranium

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:12.129
left.

00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:15.980
Many would say nuclear is only a short-term
solution to reduce carbon emissions until

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.760
truly
sustainable energy can become commonplace,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.379
but the biggest problem with nuclear energy
is not

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:27.010
the risk of meltdown, it’s not the supply
of uranium, it’s this—nuclear waste.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:31.459
All current commercial nuclear power plants
work through the process of nuclear fission.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.819
As a radioactive element decays, the individuals
atoms split into multiple, but when that happens

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:39.220
the reaction also releases energy.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.139
There are plenty of different designs of nuclear
reactors, but in

00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:46.769
general they capture the released energy by
using it to heat up water into steam which

00:02:46.769 --> 00:02:49.779
runs
through turbines that spin generators.

00:02:49.779 --> 00:02:53.030
The nuclear element used is typically uranium
which, after

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.599
about six to eight years of usage in a nuclear
power plant will have released enough of its

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:01.400
energy
that it is no longer useful in nuclear reactors,

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.830
but that doesn’t mean it’s done emitting
energy.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.330
The
fuel rods will remain radioactive enough to

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.069
emit a lethal dose for tens or hundreds of
thousands

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:11.810
of years past their removal.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.599
So the question is, what do you do with them?

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:19.519
The answer is simple—put them somewhere
where they can stay, undisturbed, isolated,

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:22.060
forever, but that’s not all that easy.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.019
In fact, no nuclear waste worldwide is currently
in what is

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:28.499
considered long-term storage.

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:32.510
Every bit of nuclear waste in existence is
in temporary storage

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.150
facilities to be used until a long-term solution
is built.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.510
Most of that nuclear waste is stored in pools
of water.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.739
Water does a decently good job of
shielding radiation so this is an inexpensive

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:46.040
and easy way of storing the rods.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.569
Usually these pools
are physically inside the nuclear power plants

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:55.019
so, when spent fuel is removed from the reactor,
it’s put directly into the water and left

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:56.019
there.

00:03:56.019 --> 00:03:58.159
The radioactive material, since it’s still
emitting

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:02.579
energy, continues to heat up the water, but
cooling systems and pumps keep the water below

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:06.279
boiling temperature, but to do that the plant
needs power.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.449
If the power fails and the backup
generators fail, the pumps and cooling systems

00:04:10.449 --> 00:04:13.730
stop working so the water heats up and can
boil

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:14.730
off.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:19.140
The water is what blocks the radiation so,
without water, the radiation just goes right

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.730
out into
the environment.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.090
In fact, exactly that happened at Fukushima.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.840
Both the primary and backup
power sources failed so the pumps and cooling

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.730
systems for the spent fuel pools couldn’t
run

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:32.140
leaving the water to heat up.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.330
The situation was brought under control before
enough water had

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.490
boiled off to release significant amounts
of radiation into the environment, but had

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.570
it not been,
thousands could have been killed.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.140
Once nuclear waste has cooled down in storage
pools for ten to twenty years, it typically

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.980
is encased in casks.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:53.540
These concrete and steel containers block
in radiation, but this solution is far

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.760
from permanent.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.100
It does not consider earthquakes, it cannot
withstand tsunamis, and it would not

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.670
work without humans.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:04.530
These casks require security and they require
maintenance.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.310
Without
humans, they could easily be damaged or breached

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:10.730
over time and release radiation into the
environment.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.100
Modern humans have only existed for about
200,000 years, so one can hardly be

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.120
sure that the species will survive for the
millions of years that the most toxic nuclear

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.380
waste will
continue to emit radiation.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.880
What’s more, one can hardly expect that
the dominant civilizations

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.220
that have nuclear technology today will continue
to exist for even thousands of years.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.940
The
Roman Empire was once without a doubt the

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.330
most powerful civilization on earth.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:38.680
Scholars even
believe that it is the most powerful civilization

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:44.120
to have ever existed on earth—more powerful
than the US, than Europe, than any modern

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.820
civilization, but it fell, and so too will
the west.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.900
Therefore, long-term nuclear waste storage
needs to last longer than any political structure,

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.500
it
needs to work without the supervision of humans,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.660
it needs to be truly and unequivocally
permanent.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.880
Finland is building just that.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.120
This region is largely devoid of natural disasters.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.560
It doesn’t
have earthquakes, it doesn’t see tsunamis,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.370
it really doesn’t encounter any natural
phenomenon

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:16.010
that could damage a nuclear waste storage
site, especially if it’s 1,500 feet underground.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.620
Beneath
an island on the Finnish Baltic Sea coast,

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:19.960
the country is digging.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.590
They’re building the very first
permanent nuclear waste storage facility in

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.200
the world in the stable bedrock 1,500 feet
below.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.560
Currently they’re just finishing their dig
down then very soon, in 2020, they’ll start

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.520
filling the
facility with nuclear waste.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.110
They’ll dig long tunnels with small holes
in which they’ll place casks

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:42.020
of nuclear waste then backfill the tunnels
with clay to be left for an eternity.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:45.010
With this system,
there’s near zero risk of nuclear material

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:48.270
leaking out into the groundwater and, once
it’s filled in

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.360
the year 2120, it can just be left, forever.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.510
Because the material will be so far down and
so

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:58.820
difficult to get to, no human management will
be necessary once completed.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:02.030
No security, no
maintenance, nothing which means it should

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.630
be truly secure, but before leaving it, they
do need

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.640
to fight against one thing—human nature.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.820
As curious beings, it’s hard to combat a
person’s urge of discovery.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:15.580
If someone finds a
mysterious structure from thousands of years

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.840
ago, it’d just be natural to want to open
it up, and

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.190
that’s a problem for nuclear waste sites.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:24.050
We essentially did just that with the pyramids
in Egypt.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:28.220
These structures were built as the final,
permanent resting places for the elites of

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:32.040
Egypt and we
opened them up because we were curious.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.210
Opening the nuclear storage facilities would
release

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:39.260
radiation into a future civilization, so we
have to tell them to leave the sites alone,

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.240
but that’s
easier said than done.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:45.070
The US Department of Energy commissioned a
study on how to communicate the danger

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:46.580
into the far future.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.600
The key is to create a message that conveys
how uninteresting, how

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.530
unimportant, and how dangerous nuclear waste
is.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:58.470
They settled on the following text:
Sending this message was important to us.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.390
We considered ourselves to be
a powerful culture.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.450
This place is not a place of honor…

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:07.280
no highly esteemed deed
is commemorated here… nothing valued is

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.280
here.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.770
What is here was dangerous and
repulsive to us.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.090
This message is a warning about danger.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.010
The danger is in a
particular location…

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:18.730
it increases toward a center… the center
of danger is here...

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.850
of a particular size and shape, and below
us.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:25.560
The danger is still present, in your
time, as it was in ours.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.250
The danger is to the body, and it can kill.

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.310
The form of the
danger is an emanation of energy.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:35.969
The danger is unleashed only if you
substantially disturb this place physically.

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:39.700
This place is best shunned and left
uninhabited.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:44.050
The idea would be to translate a message like
this into every United Nations language—

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:46.850
Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian,
and Spanish.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:51.080
There’s a reasonable hope that, at least
in the next couple thousand years, one of

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.790
those languages would be understood.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:56.250
But in the scope
of hundreds of thousands of years, there’s

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.440
just little expectation that these languages
would

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.440
survive.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.260
There’s not even a reasonable expectation
that humans would survive.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:07.780
So, you need to
convey the same message without language.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:12.090
What the study suggests is to further push
the message by building a landscape that

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.410
conveys danger.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:18.010
It could be a scene of thorns, or spikes,
or forbidding blocks.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:21.680
To satiate the
discoverer’s curiosity, it’s also suggested

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.660
to add monoliths explaining the history of
the site

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.100
through pictographs.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.090
Also included would be images like this, engraved
in stone, conveying that

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:34.550
the substance has danger that will be passed
onto humans if touched, but the difficulty

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:38.000
of this is
that it very well might not be humans exploring

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.080
earth 100,000 years from now.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.090
It could be a
species that doesn’t recognize the likeness

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:45.860
of what might be a long-extinct species.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:51.120
What some have suggested is to just let the
site be forgotten, to not mark it at all,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.200
to just
seal it up, and leave, but having something

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.010
that significant disappear isn’t simple.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:00.270
The site in
Finland is designed to not need security or

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.910
oversight, but its current location is very
well

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:06.279
documented in a potentially irreversible way.

00:10:06.279 --> 00:10:09.040
With books and brains and the internet, records
of

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.160
the site might exist until at least the end
of human civilization.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:18.750
To truly be forgotten, to truly be
left as part of nature, so too must humans

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:20.470
be forgotten.

